      <section class="lf-article-section"><h2>Structure campaigns around service intent</h2><p>Searches for emergency AC repair suggest a different need from searches for a new heat pump or a commercial HVAC contractor. Separating these services makes it easier to write relevant advertisements, choose landing pages and understand performance.</p><p>Start with a manageable set of high-priority services. Campaign segmentation should reflect real differences in intent, value, geography or conversion paths rather than creating dozens of tiny groups with little useful data.</p></section>
<section class="lf-article-section"><h2>Research keywords for the actual service area</h2><p>Potential starting themes include AC repair near me, furnace repair, air conditioning installation, HVAC replacement estimate and commercial HVAC service. These are research examples, not verified search-volume or cost-per-click estimates.</p><p>Review Google Keyword Planner, local search results and actual search-term reports before choosing targets. Different markets, seasons and match types can produce very different traffic and costs.</p></section>
<section class="lf-article-section"><h2>Use negative keywords deliberately</h2><p>HVAC search queries can include careers, training, DIY tutorials, replacement parts, repair manuals and unrelated equipment. Those searches may be unhelpful for a contractor advertising professional service appointments.</p><p>Review actual search terms and exclude irrelevant intent carefully. Avoid broad negative-keyword lists that accidentally block valuable searches, especially when terminology overlaps between professional installation and product research.</p></section>
<section class="lf-article-section"><h2>Match advertisements to useful landing pages</h2><p>A person searching for emergency cooling repair should immediately see the service offered, coverage area, contact options and expectations for the next step. Someone considering a replacement may need information about estimates, equipment evaluation and installation planning.</p><p>Make calls and forms easy to use on mobile. State availability and offers accurately. Do not fabricate customer reviews, certifications, financing approval or response-time guarantees to improve click-through rate.</p></section>
<section class="lf-article-section"><h2>Measure calls, forms and actual bookings</h2><p>Configure valid conversions for inquiry forms and appropriate tracked calls. Validate that event tags and lead routing work before increasing budgets. A button click on a booking calendar is an intent signal, not proof that an appointment was completed.</p><p>Where supported, connect CRM outcomes back to campaigns. Useful milestones include qualified service requests, confirmed appointments, attended estimates and closed jobs, while respecting consent and privacy requirements.</p></section>
<section class="lf-article-section"><h2>Optimize for economics, not the cheapest lead</h2><p>A repair campaign and a replacement campaign can have different ticket sizes, margins, sales cycles and close rates. Compare cost per qualified lead, cost per booked estimate and customer acquisition cost by service type.</p><p>Use actual business data to make budget decisions. There is no universal HVAC cost-per-lead target that applies to every service, city and season. Increase spend only when sales capacity and lead quality support it.</p></section>

      <section class="lf-article-section">
        <h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>
        <h3>What keywords work for HVAC Google Ads?</h3><p>Service-specific commercial searches matching your capabilities and service area are a good starting point. Validate demand and intent before spending.</p>
<h3>Should HVAC contractors optimize for phone calls or forms?</h3><p>It depends on urgency and the service offered. Repairs may favor calls, while replacements and commercial projects can benefit from structured inquiries and estimate scheduling.</p>
